        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-17" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Q1" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="60" x="200" y="110" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-18" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="Q3" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="60" x="170" y="580" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-20" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 12px;&quot;&gt;How does one think about grounding an a feature dimension?&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;A feature dimensions value is set to a quantity X if it is set to the same quantity in other similar contexts. A context&#39;s similarity can be arbitrarily defined and depends on the problem to be solved.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;E.g. a feature for &quot;Julienning&quot; is set to true for query Q1 because it is similar to A. It is true for query Q2 as it similar to D (in terms of text description). Similarly for Q3 --&amp;gt; C.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A system must be trained to recognize that Q2 is related to the attribute. It does NOT happen automatically.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="210" width="430" x="-190" y="720" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-24" edge="1" parent="1" source="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-22" style="edgeStyle=orthogonalEdgeStyle;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=1;jettySize=auto;html=1;entryX=1;entryY=0.5;entryDx=0;entryDy=0;" target="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-4">
          <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-22" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;overflow=hidden;rounded=0;" value="&lt;h1 style=&quot;margin-top: 0px;&quot;&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 12px;&quot;&gt;What is the Julienning technique?&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Julienne cutting method, also known as “matchstick cutting,” involves slicing ingredients into long, thin strips, typically measuring about 1/8 inch in thickness. This technique is named after the French word “Julienne,” which refers to a type of soup made with thinly sliced vegetables. The Julienne cut is commonly used in various cuisines, including French, Asian, and Italian cooking, to prepare a wide range of dishes, from stir-fries and sautés to salads and garnishes.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="250" width="320" x="730" y="750" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="0hWZ1rmW96yIc9XrhgOy-25" parent="1" style="text;html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;rounded=0;" value="D" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="30" width="60" x="640" y="850" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
